<h2>The anatomy of the settlement</h2>
<p>The wife&rsquo;s allegations covered the familiar matrimonial span — dowry demands, cruelty and retention of stridhan. What the mediation produced was less familiar in its discipline. The headline figure of Rs 1.5 crore was not an undifferentiated payout: Rs 1 crore was earmarked for the welfare and education of the two sons, converting the larger part of the settlement from spousal compensation into child provision. Custody went to the mother, with the husband relinquishing claims to custody and visitation. The divorce followed under Section 13B of the Hindu Marriage Act, recorded on 9 April 2026, and the final tranche — a Rs 50 lakh demand draft — was handed over before the Court at the quashing hearing.</p>

<h2>The one difficult charge</h2>
<p>Four of the five offences sat comfortably within the settled settlement-quashing framework of <em>Gian Singh v. State of Punjab</em>, <em>Narinder Singh v. State of Punjab</em> and <em>Jitendra Raghuvanshi v. Babita Raghuvanshi</em>: predominantly private, matrimonial in origin, with conviction prospects collapsing once the complainant stood by the settlement. The outlier was Section 354A IPC — sexual harassment — chargesheeted against one petitioner over a single incident.</p>
<p>Justice Datta addressed it directly rather than by silence. The settlement expressly covered that petitioner; the complainant characterised the episode as a misunderstanding arising from the same marital discord; and the prosecution&rsquo;s case rested on her testimony. Continuing the trial of that count alone, the Court held, &ldquo;would be an exercise in futility.&rdquo; The reasoning matters for practice: offences against dignity are not quashed as a courtesy to the settlement — they are quashed, when they are, because the evidentiary foundation has genuinely dissolved.</p>

<h2>Costs as the new normal</h2>
<div class="note">The quashing came with costs of Rs 1,00,000, payable to the Delhi High Court Legal Services Committee. The signal is consistent across recent Delhi practice: parties who set the criminal law in motion and later resolve the dispute privately should expect to contribute something to the system whose resources the prosecution consumed. Settlement remains welcome; cost-free settlement is no longer assumed.</div>

<h2>Sequencing lessons for matrimonial settlements</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Mediate comprehensively.</strong> The MOU should name every proceeding — FIR, DV case, divorce, custody, execution — and state exactly what happens to each, with the quantum staged against those milestones.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Record the divorce first.</strong> The Section 13B decree crystallises the separation and demonstrates to the quashing court that the settlement is genuine and performed, not tactical.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Keep the final tranche for the quashing hearing.</strong> Handing over the closing draft in court — as happened here — gives the judge visible assurance of performance and the paying side protection against a settlement that closes nothing.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Deal with every charge expressly.</strong> Where the chargesheet includes offences against dignity, the settlement should cover the concerned accused in terms, and counsel should be ready to show why the evidence cannot survive the complainant&rsquo;s own position.</div>
</div>

<h2>The larger picture</h2>
<p>The decision continues a steady Delhi line in which the criminal case is treated as the last open file of a concluded matrimonial dispute. The courts&rsquo; consistent conditions are visible here in miniature: a settlement that is genuine, performed and comprehensive; a complainant who affirms it in person; children whose interests are secured rather than bargained away; and, increasingly, a contribution to legal services funds as the price of closure.</p>
